.0096435546875, -0.031494140625, 0.00936126708984375, 0.02801513671875, -0.004638671875, 0.007274627685546875, 0.0151519775390625, -0.01502227783203125, -0.0152435302734375, 0.0259246826171875, 0.0009479522705078125, 0.039825439453125, 0.007434844970703125, 0.006092071533203125, -0.02264404296875, 0.036590576171875, 0.0198822021484375, 0.0021419525146484375, 0.0093536376953125, -0.0207672119140625, -0.01190185546875, 0.0005869865417480469, -0.011749267578125, -0.0189666748046875, 0.0277862548828125, 0.0010137557983398438, 0.031402587890625, -0.020477294921875, 0.004657745361328125, -0.0015392303466796875, -0.0009927749633789062, 0.022186279296875, -0.018402099609375, 0.00485992431640625, -0.0216827392578125, -0.0292816162109375, 0.03741455078125, -0.028350830078125, -0.021514892578125, 0.01526641845703125, 0.002307891845703125, 0.017913818359375, 0.0171661376953125, 0.03826904296875, 0.031585693359375, -0.01476287841796875, -0.01325225830078125, 0.0003724098205566406], "payload": {"model": "text-embedding-3-small", "text": "Purpose: To develop and evaluate a locally deployed multi-agent AI system for radiology report structuring and quality assurance.\n\nMaterials and Methods: This retrospective study included 638 radiology reports from CT examinations of the chest, abdomen, and pelvis dictated by 15 board-certified radiologists in 2023 and 2024. A multi-agent AI pipeline was developed and deployed locally to perform report structuring and quality assurance (QA). The pipeline consisted of three primary agents: (1) a Sectioning Agent, which used regular expression (regex) rules and a local large language model (LLM) to segment reports into standardized anatomical sections at the sentence level; (2) a Consistency Agent, which compared content between the Findings and Impression sections and within sections to detect mismatches; and (3) a QA Agent, which identified gender-anatomy conflicts and checked for documentation of critical findings and their communication. The agents operated sequentially, with outputs from the Sectioning Agent feeding into the Consistency Agent, followed by the QA Agent. The workflow was orchestrated to ensure each report passed through all agents, and flagged issues were aggregated for review.\n\nA subset of 45 reports was selected for independent evaluation by two board-certified radiologists. The subset was chosen using stratified random sampling to ensure representation of all flagged error types (section mismatches, gender-anatomy conflicts, and undocumented critical findings) and unflagged reports, proportional to their prevalence in the full dataset.\n\nEvaluation Metrics: \"Correctly restructured\" was defined as the report being segmented into anatomical sections without altering the original clinical content or meaning. \"Incorrectly restructured\" indicated that the restructuring introduced errors in section assignment or altered the intended meaning. QA performance ratings were assigned on a 3-point scale: \"excellent\" (no errors and all relevant issues correctly flagged), \"good\" (minor errors or omissions not affecting clinical interpretation), and \"fair\" (errors or omissions with potential to affect clinical interpretation). Reviewer agreement was assessed using Cohen\u2019s kappa statistic, and 95% confidence intervals (CIs) were calculated for key proportions.\n\nResults:\n\nSystem Performance\n\n- The multi-agent system structured the Findings sections of all 638 reports (22,270 sentences) into a predefined anatomical format while retaining the original report content.\n- The system flagged 90 reports (14.1%) for at least one QA issue:\n    - Section mismatches: 80 reports (12.5%)\n    - Gender-anatomy conflicts: 7 reports (1.1%)\n    - Undocumented communication of critical findings: 3 reports (0.5%)\n\nRadiologist Evaluation\n\n- Of the 45-report evaluation subset, both reviewers agreed that 31 reports (69%; 95% CI: 54\u201381%) were correctly restructured, 2 reports (4%; 95% CI: 0\u201313%) were incorrectly restructured, and they disagreed on 12 reports (27%; 95% CI: 15\u201341%). Inter-rater agreement for restructuring was moderate (Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.56).\n- For QA flagging, both reviewers agreed that no clinically important information was omitted and no fabricated content was introduced in any report.\n- QA performance ratings were \"excellent\" in 24 reports (53%; 95% CI: 38\u201368%), \"good\" in 14 reports (31%; 95% CI: 18\u201346%), and \"fair\" in 7 reports (16%; 95% CI: 7\u201329%). Inter-rater agreement for QA ratings was substantial (Cohen\u2019s kappa = 0.72).\n\nDetailed QA Task Results\n\n- Of the 7 reports flagged for gender-anatomy conflicts, both reviewers confirmed 6 as true positives and 1 as a false positive.\n- Of the 3 reports flagged for undocumented communication of critical findings, both reviewers agreed all were true positives.\n- For section mismatches, 75 of 80 flagged reports were confirmed as true positives by at least one reviewer.\n\nLimitations\n\nThis study was limited by its retrospective design and single-institution dataset, which may affect generalizability. The AI system\u2019s performance may vary with different report styles or institutions. The evaluation subset, while stratified, was relatively small. Reviewer assessments may be subject to individual interpretation, and the system\u2019s reliance on regex and LLMs may not capture all possible report variations.\n\nConclusion: A locally deployed multi-agent AI system was developed to combine radiology report structuring and quality assurance within a single workflow. The system achieved high accuracy in anatomical sectioning and effective detection of QA issues, as confirmed by radiologist evaluation and inter-rater agreement statistics. Such systems have the potential to improve standardization and quality assurance in radiology reporting."}}